    {
      "id": "gate_slam",
      "category": "impact",
      "notes": "The keep's iron gate swung shut: a sequence, as a heavy gate closes. The recording is a wrought-iron gate (FSD50K 54269, CC0): its closing squeal, strike and latch, played 300 cents lower, so slower and larger. It was recorded outdoors with steady noise about 25 dB under the gate, between 60 Hz and 1 kHz: a high-pass at 1 kHz under the squeal (which has nothing below it) opens to 300 Hz for the strike, and the quiet gap and the end of the ring are faded out, so no noise is heard alone. The recorded gate is light, so the weight is synthesized and unpitched, placed on the measured onsets: under the strike at 1.3506 s a thump falling to 36 Hz, a burst of low noise for the frame's mass and a rumble; under the latch at 1.6387 s a smaller thump. Nothing in it rings at a pitch: long, pitched modes made the first versions sound like a bell. Variants move only 10 cents, so the recording's strike stays within about 6 ms of its thump. Needs FSD50K's evaluation set (render --samples).",
