      "style": false,
      "notes": "The sea on the rocks below the walls: two waves in each loop. Each wave's body builds and opens up as it rises and breaks (a roar of noise whose low-pass rises from 400 Hz to 2.5 kHz at the break, then closes as the wave falls back), the break throws up foam, a hiss of high noise that peaks 0.2 s after the break and dies away in about 1.5 s (exponentially: 17 dB), and the surf's low rumble runs under it all. The foam is noise rather than bubbles: bubbles at fewer than about ten thousand a second are heard one by one, as drips (van den Doel 2004). Every layer is a bed that ends where it starts, so the loop joins itself.",
